            <ArticleTitle>Sex differences in perceiving analgesic drug effects as measured by subjective pain ratings: a concealed signal detection theory analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>761-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a study using metamizole and placebo as equivalents of a strong and a weak stimulus in a signal detection device female headaches patients show better ability to discriminate the two drug stimuli than males, whereas there is no significant effect with respect to response bias.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
